  "titleEn": "Urgent Mission",
  "titleEs": "Misión urgente",
  "titleText": {
    "es": "Sí, podríamos detener a los dictadores y las pandemias, pero también podríamos hacer que tengan sentido los signos de todos los malditos diagramas.",
    "en": "Sure, we could stop dictators and pandemics, but we could also make the signs on every damn diagram make sense.",
    "noteEn": "The Spanish archive closely translates the official title text. It renders ‘make sense’ with hacer que tengan sentido and ‘every damn diagram’ with todos los malditos diagramas; this wording is not printed inside the strip."
  },
  "cardDefinitions": [
    {
      "id": "word-del--from-time",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "del",
      "answerEn": "from the (a time or period)",
      "noteEn": "Del combines de and el. Here de marks a time or period as the source something comes from, rather than showing ownership.",
      "example": { "es": "Recibimos una carta del pasado.", "en": "We received a letter from the past." },
      "tags": ["word", "contraction", "time", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-convención--agreed-rule",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "convención",
      "answerEn": "convention; an agreed rule or standard",
      "noteEn": "Here convención means an agreed way of naming or handling something, not an organized gathering.",
      "example": { "es": "La convención usa números positivos.", "en": "The convention uses positive numbers." },
      "tags": ["word", "agreed rule", "technical vocabulary", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-estableciendo--setting-convention",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "estableciendo",
      "answerEn": "setting; establishing",
      "noteEn": "Estableciendo is the ongoing-action form of establecer, ‘to establish.’ It ends in -iendo and is used here with a form of estar.",
      "example": { "es": "Está estableciendo una regla clara.", "en": "She is establishing a clear rule." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "ongoing action", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-carga--electric-charge",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "carga",
      "answerEn": "electric charge",
      "noteEn": "In physics, carga is the electrical property described as positive or negative. This is distinct from a physical load or an explosive charge.",
      "example": { "es": "La partícula tiene carga positiva.", "en": "The particle has positive charge." },
      "tags": ["word", "physics", "electric charge", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-eléctrica",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "eléctrica",
      "answerEn": "electric; electrical (feminine singular)",
      "noteEn": "Eléctrica is the feminine singular form of eléctrico and matches a feminine singular noun.",
      "example": { "es": "La corriente eléctrica es débil.", "en": "The electric current is weak." },
      "tags": ["word", "adjective", "feminine singular", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-al--fixed-al-reves",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "al",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; part of al revés (‘backward’ or ‘upside down’)",
      "noteEn": "Al normally combines a and el, but al revés is learned as a fixed expression.",
      "example": { "es": "La foto está al revés.", "en": "The photo is upside down." },
      "tags": ["word", "fixed expression", "al revés", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-revés--backward",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "revés",
      "answerEn": "backward; the wrong way around (as part of al revés)",
      "noteEn": "In the fixed expression al revés, revés says that the orientation, order, or direction is reversed.",
      "example": { "es": "Puso la etiqueta al revés.", "en": "He put the label on backward." },
      "tags": ["word", "fixed expression", "reversed orientation", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-deja--leaves-deposit",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "deja",
      "answerEn": "leaves; deposits",
      "noteEn": "Deja can also mean ‘lets’ or ‘allows.’ Here it is a form of dejar meaning that a material leaves something behind on a surface.",
      "example": { "es": "La tiza deja polvo en la mesa.", "en": "The chalk leaves dust on the table." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "leaving a trace", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-seda",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "seda",
      "answerEn": "silk",
      "noteEn": "",
      "tags": ["word", "material", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-vidrio",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "vidrio",
      "answerEn": "glass (the material)",
      "noteEn": "",
      "tags": ["word", "material", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-negativa--charge-sign",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "negativa",
      "answerEn": "negative (feminine singular; an electric-charge sign)",
      "noteEn": "Negativa ends in -a to match the feminine noun carga. In this physics context, it names one of the two conventional charge signs.",
      "example": { "es": "La carga de un electrón es negativa.", "en": "An electron's charge is negative." },
      "tags": ["word", "adjective", "physics", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-íbamos--planned-action",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "íbamos",
      "answerEn": "we were going; we were planning (to do something)",
      "noteEn": "Íbamos is a past form of ir. Before a and an unchanged action verb, it can present a plan viewed from the past, often one that was interrupted or not carried out.",
      "example": { "es": "Íbamos a llamar mañana.", "en": "We were going to call tomorrow." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "past plan", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-del--fixed-maquina-del-tiempo",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "del",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; part of máquina del tiempo (‘time machine’)",
      "noteEn": "Del combines de and el. Spanish keeps it between máquina and tiempo in the conventional expression máquina del tiempo.",
      "example": { "es": "La máquina del tiempo desapareció.", "en": "The time machine disappeared." },
      "tags": ["word", "contraction", "fixed expression", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-apocalipsis",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "apocalipsis",
      "answerEn": "apocalypse",
      "noteEn": "Apocalipsis is masculine in Spanish: el apocalipsis.",
      "example": { "es": "La novela imagina un apocalipsis.", "en": "The novel imagines an apocalypse." },
      "tags": ["word", "noun", "masculine", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-robótico",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "robótico",
      "answerEn": "robotic (masculine singular)",
      "noteEn": "Robótico describes something related to robots and matches a masculine singular noun.",
      "example": { "es": "Diseñaron un brazo robótico.", "en": "They designed a robotic arm." },
      "tags": ["word", "adjective", "robots", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-construyó",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "construyó",
      "answerEn": "built; constructed",
      "noteEn": "Construyó presents one completed past act by he, she, or formal you. Its written accent distinguishes it from construyo, ‘I build.’",
      "example": { "es": "El equipo construyó un prototipo.", "en": "The team built a prototype." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb", "completed past", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-tratarse-de-tema",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "tratarse de + tema · ¿de qué se trata?",
      "questionEn": "How do you say what a situation is about, or ask what it is about?",
      "answerEn": "Use se trata de before the topic; ask ¿de qué se trata? when the topic is unknown.",
      "noteEn": "The plus sign means ‘followed by.’ In this expression, se and de stay fixed while trata can change for time, as in se trataba de for a past topic. The expression does not name a person who performs tratar.",
      "example": { "es": "¿De qué se trata la película?", "en": "What is the film about?" },
      "tags": ["phrase", "topic", "question", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-estar-al-reves",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "estar al revés",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that something is backward, upside down, or the wrong way around?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of estar followed by the fixed expression al revés.",
      "noteEn": "Estar changes to match the person or thing and the time: está al revés, estaban al revés. Al revés stays unchanged; its exact English wording depends on whether the problem is orientation, order, or direction.",
      "example": { "es": "Los números están al revés.", "en": "The numbers are backward." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "estar", "orientation", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-deberia-normative-state",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "debería + infinitivo · estado correcto o deseable",
      "questionEn": "How do you say what should be true because it is the correct or desired state?",
      "answerEn": "Use debería, or another matching form of deber ending in -ría, then the next verb in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "noteEn": "The unchanged dictionary form ends in -ar, -er, or -ir. This use judges what would be correct or desirable rather than predicting what is likely. Only deber changes for the subject: debería ser for one thing, deberían ser for several things.",
      "example": { "es": "La salida debería ser accesible.", "en": "The exit should be accessible." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "deber", "correct state", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-maquina-del-tiempo",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "máquina del tiempo",
      "questionEn": "What is the usual Spanish expression for a machine that travels through time?",
      "answerEn": "Use máquina del tiempo.",
      "noteEn": "Del combines de and el. The expression keeps del tiempo after máquina; Spanish does not normally build this noun pair by placing tiempo directly before máquina.",
      "example": { "es": "La novela incluye una máquina del tiempo.", "en": "The novel includes a time machine." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "science fiction", "time travel", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-franklin-electric-charge-sign-convention",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "Benjamín Franklin · signos de la carga eléctrica",
      "answerEn": "Benjamin Franklin introduced the positive and negative labels for electric charge. By the modern convention, glass rubbed with silk is positive; the visitor wants those labels reversed.",
      "noteEn": "The labels predate the discovery of electrons. Conventional current follows the direction assigned to positive charge, while electrons in metal move the other way, so the signs can feel backward in circuit work. The joke is that an electrical engineer uses the time machine to change this convention instead of preventing a robot apocalypse.",
      "tags": ["concept", "Benjamin Franklin", "electric charge", "physics joke"]
    }
  ],
